{
  "items": [
    {
      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "Mei's AP Biology class in Boise measured the activity of catalase, an enzyme in potato tissue that breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen gas. Each group placed 5 grams of potato in 10 mL of 3% hydrogen peroxide held at one set temperature and measured the oxygen collected over 60 seconds. The table shows the class averages.\n\n| Temperature (°C) | Rate of O2 production (mL per minute) |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 10 | 1.2 |\n| 20 | 2.5 |\n| 30 | 4.8 |\n| 37 | 6.1 |\n| 45 | 7.0 |\n| 55 | 3.2 |\n| 65 | 0.4 |\n\n",
      "question": "(a) Identify the independent variable and the dependent variable in the experiment. (b) Describe the relationship between temperature and the rate of oxygen production shown in the data, including the temperature at which the rate is highest. (c) Explain, using the structure of the enzyme, why the rate drops sharply between 45 °C and 65 °C. (d) Mei's group repeats the experiment at 37 °C with a competitive inhibitor added to the hydrogen peroxide. Predict how the rate of oxygen production would compare with the uninhibited rate at 37 °C, and justify your prediction.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Variables",
          "description": "Identifies temperature as the independent variable and rate of oxygen production (mL per minute) as the dependent variable.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Trend",
          "description": "Describes the rate rising from 10 °C to a peak near 45 °C, then falling sharply by 65 °C, citing values from the table.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Enzyme structure",
          "description": "Explains that high temperature breaks the hydrogen and other bonds that hold the enzyme in its folded shape, changing the active site so the substrate binds poorly or not at all.",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Prediction",
          "description": "Predicts that the rate with the competitive inhibitor is lower than the uninhibited rate at 37 °C.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Justification",
          "description": "Justifies the prediction by explaining that the inhibitor competes with hydrogen peroxide for the active site, so fewer enzyme molecules bind substrate at a given time.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "Independent variable is temperature; dependent variable is rate of oxygen production in mL per minute",
        "Rate rises from 10 °C to a peak near 45 °C, then drops sharply to 0.4 mL per minute at 65 °C",
        "High temperature disrupts hydrogen and other bonds, denaturing the enzyme and altering the active site",
        "A competitive inhibitor occupies the active site, blocking substrate binding and lowering the rate at 37 °C"
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) Independent variable: temperature (°C). Dependent variable: rate of O2 production (mL per minute). (b) The rate increases from 1.2 mL per minute at 10 °C to a peak of 7.0 mL per minute at 45 °C, then falls to 3.2 at 55 °C and 0.4 at 65 °C. (c) Above its optimum, heat breaks the bonds that maintain the enzyme's tertiary structure, so the active site loses its shape and fewer hydrogen peroxide molecules can bind, slowing the reaction. (d) The competitive inhibitor resembles the substrate and binds the active site, so fewer enzyme molecules hold substrate at once and the rate at 37 °C will be lower than 6.1 mL per minute."
    }
  ]
}

